hey guys Alexa right here standing in  for Peter Brown let's talk about Tony  Hawk's Pro Skater 5 Tony Hawk's Pro  Skater 5 momentarily recalls the  greatness of earlier games in the series  skating around and looking for  opportunities to test your creativity  and reflexes is sometimes as enjoyable  today as it was 10 15 years ago  however these moments of excitement are  fleeting the core mechanics are there  but the supporting systems are  half-baked and rickety for anyone who's  played Tony Hawk games in the past  skating pulling off tricks and forming  combos in Tony Hawk pro skater 5 will  come naturally and it's accessible for  newcomers too however the same button  that's used to grind is also tied to the  new slam action which forces your skater  and their board downward you can use it  to your advantage if you've missed time  the distance between takeoff and landing  during a combo but you can no longer  buffer a grind in midair because you  instantly plummet to the ground  of course skating sessions are only as  good as the locations they take place in  and the eight maps in Tony Hawk pro  skater 5 are too small lacking and  interesting opportunities they're also  ugly with poorly drawn textures and  constant texture pop-in pulling off  combos often requires identifying a  series of loosely connected objects that  you can grind on between air based  tricks and there are a few cleverly  hidden wants to be found but nowhere  near as many as in past games some maps  will frustrate you in other ways beyond  their lack of interesting elements it's  all too easy to hit a ramp or jump off  other objects only to find that you've  gone out of bounds Tony Hawk's Pro  Skater 5 also features a shakey cam that  activates when you ride over rough  terrain or whenever the game feels like  it frame rates are also an issue and  it's not uncommon that you fall through  geometry clip through it and ruin a  combo or fall victim to anomalous  physics course progressing from map to  map requires that you collect stars by  performing various feats and completing  challenges these can include reaching a  high score collecting items or pushing  balls out of a pool among a few others  these tasks very little from map to map  and they are indeed tasks because they  never deliver an enjoyable or  challenging experience  your only motivation to play them are  these stars to unlock maps and skill  points that can be used to increase your  abilities though they never make a  tangible difference your skater feels  the same on day one as he does on day 5  Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 5 is built as an  online game but it does little to entice  you into playing with others according  to Peter he was never paired with more  than one other person outside a free  skate  were times when a multiplayer challenge  would start with him as the sole  participant online connectivity gets in  the way during single-player missions -  if you're connected to the Internet when  you're trying to play single-player Tony  Hawk pro skater 5 still tries to connect  to a server if it has any problems  during this process you have to wait for  it to timeout disconnect your console  from the internet and try again  beyond the boring levels and challenges  Tony Hawk pro skater 5 has another big  problem with organization and  presentation when scrolling through the  main menu you're presented with a few  options levels my levels and player  created levels the game teases you with  more to do by placing arrows on the top  and bottom of the list as though you can  scroll to see more but it does the same  thing on the map selection screen  creating the illusion that there's more  to the game than there actually is once  you're playing a challenge there's no  way to exit it besides returning to the  game's main menu and even if you stick  with it and wait for the time when it  took away you'll have to endure a series  of loading screens before you can head  back to skating Peter tried his hardest  to engage with the activities in Tony  Hawk's Pro Skater 5 to eke out enjoyment  but problems with menus loading screens  and glitches made it all too easy to put  down the controller and walk away within  Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 5 lies a basic  skating game that's difficult to enjoy  because you have to jump over numerous  hoops and ignore a plethora of obvious  issues to find the smallest amount of  fun previous Tony Hawk games were able  to capture your imagination and motivate  you through character progression here  interesting levels and even a great  soundtrack the soundtrack in this game  is good enough but it's riddled with  technical glitches and design missteps  making it a huge step back for the  series
 

